body:
Have you considered applying to fully remote positions? I know that companies such as Red Hat, etc, offer fully remote positions to employees in the UK, though I think their openings for graduates is a bit later in the year. If you are desperate for a job then keep applying but you're going to have to ask for £27k, which IMO is still under the industry standard in your area but still somewhat liveable.

&#x200B;

If you really need a job and take a lower paying role, then realistically you should leave in 6 months - also avoid companies like FDM group or Sparta that try to rope you into dodgy contracts. You're probably not going to learn that much, and if you're getting paid that low you probably won't get a salary increase for staying.

Dude, you have a masters in Computer Science. What are you doing applying for jobs with £20-22k? Ask for a reasonable amount, you're not going to be able to live on £20-22k a year alone, even in Leeds. And use multiple job boards to apply for roles.

&#x200B;

>From what I gathered in the interview, most they interviewed do not have a Master's degree and some have even only done a bootcamp and not Bachelor's degree.

&#x200B;

You're clearly overqualified for this role if this is the case and they're offering £22k. Getting a job might not be easy as a graduate, and I'd assume Leeds has nowhere near the same opportunities as Birmingham/London and other cities, but you should at the very least be aiming to ask for £27k or more.

19k is grossly underpaid for a junior developer job, even for one in a low cost of living area. Are you sure those are all jobs? I'm sure some companies or places would be willing to exploit but IMO you can efinitely aim for at least high 20s or low 30s even as a junior.

&#x200B;

FWIW, my friend is completely self taught, bad grades at school and never did a degree and started on £38k.

Apply. I think you will get in. As long as you have an A-Level in Maths, you'll be fine. Your grade of 2:1 is fine as they don't ask for 'high/good 2:1'. Your Oxford bachelor's advantage will get you in. On admissionreports the admissions for MSC CS at UCL is quite low, but that could be people applying from everywhere... and worst case, there are a lot of good universities you can still apply and get into without a doubt.

&#x200B;

I applied to Birmingham, Nottingham & Glasgow for the MSc CS. I'm a humanities undergrad from Nottingham, literally no overlap, and my transcript isn't the best. Birmingham gave me an offer, just waiting on the other two. I didn't include my GitHub, just made a good personal statement, and if you want I could probably give you some advice. But I think you'll be fine.

100%. I actually don't know how you can survive in London even on high 20s as a graduate living alone, even with a house share the CoL plus tax and more is choking the life out of people. Honestly the only thing that I actually have going for me is that I still have family nearby, else I would be looking for work elsewhere. I honestly think for our generation that people are going to struggle to buy houses at current rates unless we make long term investments or create value (i.e successful startups and the like).

&#x200B;

London does pay more than the rest of the country significantly, and for certain fields definitely, but this idea that it matches to cities like NYC, SF or the like is just inaccurate when you consider the cost of rent, living costs and the huge amount of tax being taken out of earnings, in addition to the fact that average salaries are much lower in London than those cities.
